it's great to be joined today by Kara  brown McCormack who's the co-founder of  the committee for ranked choice voting  in Maine which had a big big success  when it comes to the the fight for  ranked choice voting kara great to talk  to you today it's great to talk to you  David thanks so much for having me so we  recently talked to fair vote about what  ranked choice voting is different  methods of installing ranked choice  voting how votes can be tabulated so we  have sort of a sense of how it works  what I'd like to talk about with you to  begin with is how did this initiative in  Maine start in other words how did this  activism become successful activism  well we started in Maine with about four  years ago with about five people and  what we did is we filled out a piece of  paper and turned it in to the Secretary  of State with a copy of the law that we  wanted to put forward which was a ranked  choice voting law for all federal and  state offices in the state of Maine we  by that action were able to get the  Secretary of State to approve a petition  and we spent about a year with  volunteers gathering 75,000 signatures  we needed sixty 1123 signatures which  represents 10% of the people who voted  in the last election or in that case it  would have been in the 2012 election and  we then had a campaign a year-long  campaign and in November of 2016 we won  ranked choice voting in Maine with the  second largest vote in the history of  citizens initiatives in Maine so to kind  of outline the process in its pieces  first you sort of drafted what the law  would look like once that was sort of  okayed conceptually at that point you  needed signatures from Maine residents  in order to get it on the ballot as a  referendum question as a as a citizen  initiated referendum question exactly  ultimately voted on in 2016 and  past it wasn't a huge margin it was 52  to 48 but that made it the law and how  soon after that did it go into effect so  it went into effect on January 7th of  2017 that's when it became the law of  the state of Maine and can then I'm  sorry yeah go ahead well I'm just gonna  say and then about four days later we  realized that the Republican state  Senate President was going to begin the  process of repealing the citizens  initiative in the legislature because a  citizen's law doesn't have any more  weight than a law that's created by the  legislature so it's not like just  because it was a citizen's initiative  and we passed it into law with you know  the vote of the people that it was  gold-plated in anyway it just it's just  a law than like any other law and so we  wrongly assumed that we were done and  what happened over the next ten months  is really really interesting we fought  off one repeal attempt in the  legislature at the end of June of 2017  and the government was even shut down  and repealing rank-choice voting was one  of the conditions that the governor of  Maine put on his list of conditions for  reopening up the government  it didn't get repealed then but then on  October 23rd of 2017 in a special  session a late-night session of the  legislature that was called specifically  for this purpose the legislature I would  say you know most Republicans and some  Democrats combined to repeal the  citizen's initiative what were the  reasons that they cited for their  opposition to this they you know  different people had different reasons  for for opposing ranked choice voting  there the Secretary of State said that  it was too costly that it was too  confusing people said things like well  the people didn't really know  they were voting for or it's a lot of  people said it was unconstitutional  there was there were there were many  many objections from the political  establishment in Maine and they the  legislature had you know fundamentally  altered or repealed all of the citizens  initiatives that we passed in November  of 2016 so rank-choice voting wasn't  alone in in that but it was the first  time I think that a citizen's initiative  had been in the history of me and that a  citizen's initiative had been sort of on  its you know in its whole repeal so  what's next for the for the movement I  mean where do things stand now and what  is it that we should be looking to do  well this is really interesting so what  happened on October 23rd was that they  repealed it but that night we watched  them from the gallery repeal the law and  we launched that night on October 23rd  2017 what's called a people's veto so we  have in our Constitution something  besides just a citizen's initiative we  can also take away from the legislature  something that we deem repellant as a  people so in 88 days in the middle of  last winter we collected another sixty  1123 signatures and we turned them in to  the Secretary of State on February 2nd  of 2018 and we froze our original law in  place by that action and so rank-choice  voting was implemented and used for the  first time in Maine on June 12th of 2018  and then we voted on that same day this  time we won by an 8% margin to keep  rank-choice voting so we used it again  in November this past November election  for the first time ever in the United  States of America rank-choice voting was  used for federal offices in a general  election statewide in the state of Maine  so it wasn't over when they repealed  that it actually just made us sort of  stronger and more resilient can you talk  a little bit about whether there are  races do we even know whether there are  races that would have  turned out differently if ranked choice  voting were not in place in other words  would some of the recent races voted on  under rank choice would would the  results have been different if  first-past-the-post were still in place  well I can tell you that in in the  second congressional district of Maine  we only have two Congress people from  the state of Maine we're a small state  and in the second congressional district  ranked choice voting was used so the  Republican incumbent congressman Bruce  Poliquin received 46% of the vote and  the Democratic challenger Jared golden  also received 46% of the vote and then  two independents ran in that race and  they both got about twelve percent of  the vote all together and so I'm sorry  they both split the rest of the rest of  that vote and so in the first round of  counting the incumbent congressman had a  you know margin on the  first-past-the-post he he got the most  first choice votes but once the ranked  choice voting kicked in  Jared golden emerged the winner and will  be sworn into Congress at the beginning  of next year absolutely amazing so in  other words you by virtue of having rank  choice voting in that race  individuals who felt more aligned with  one of the third party candidates could  vote for those third party candidates to  show support but they were not  ultimately helping to elect the person  with whom they most disagreed because on  the second tally their votes transferred  to the eventual winner yes you totally  understand so the people who were into  voting for the independence more of them  chose Jared gold in the Democrat as  their second choice then Bruce Poliquin  and so Jared golden emerged with more  than 50% of the vote in the first in the  second round of counting in the  tabulation and he was elected with a  majority of the vote and with the  broadest  you know the broadest number of people  and that's how rank-choice voting is  meant to work you can vote for the  person that you like the most without  fear of electing the person that you  like the least  and the candidate who wins is elected  with the majority of the people and we  hope will govern in a different in a  different way and on behalf of the  broadest number of his or her  constituents are you at all concerned  about the possibility of main  rank-choice voting being overturned in  some other way the Maine Supreme Court  questions of constitutionality anything  like that so we're actually in court  right now in the United States District  Court Poliquin when he didn't win the  election went took us to court and said  that the election should be thrown out  that it was an illegal election that the  federal court should immediately declare  him the winner and throw out there was  you know he wanted to stop the counting  and and have the court immediately  declared him the winner and he asked for  a temporary restraining order in federal  court a couple of weeks ago the judge  who was appointed by donald trump just  just over a month ago this was one of  his first cases to be heard he ruled  against bruce poliquin in the temporary  restraining order he said that there  were no constitutional United States  constitutional issues with ranked choice  voting he allowed bruce poliquin lawsuit  to proceed and yesterday we were in  court for another five hours up in  bangor in the US District Court and the  arguments were made by our side and by  poliquin's side and we'll see we the  judge told us that we should have a  final ruling by next week and we expect  that the judge will rule in our favor  and that the people of Maine have a  right under the United States  Constitution to choose the way that we  elect our leaders and we look very much  forward to that decision because  hopefully it will pave the way if others  if other people in other states want to  bring rank-choice voting to their  politics that that Court decision will  help them do it because rank-choice  voting is not unconstitutional  what other B you mentioned other states  are there where else should people be  looking now for the strongest  initiatives to do rank-choice voting at  the state level so I think that that's a  question you know that's sort of yet to  be answered I know that in Massachusetts  and in in Wisconsin there are some  efforts and for sure in the state of  Minnesota Minnesota has ranked choice  voting in both Minneapolis and st. Paul  and there's a fabulous organization  there called fair vote Minnesota and a  woman called gene Massey who's leading  an effort there which I think is very  very strong because they already use it  in two of their biggest cities in the  state and you know the reason why Maine  had such a good chance to get ranked  choice voting although it was a huge  struggle is because we use it in the  city of Portland Maine and so we've used  it in the city of Portland Maine since  2011 so it wasn't a completely foreign  concept to the people here can you talk  a little bit in the last bit of time we  have left  what other policy coupled with rank  choice voting do you think would most  significantly change or improve upon  some of the problems we have in the  United States so would it be rank choice  voting coupled with for example public  financing of Elections would it be  ranked choice voting coupled with a  repeal or undoing of citizens united for  example or I mean really whatever it is  what other policy could build on rank  choice voting to really improve the  democratic process so I think that rank  choice voting is the most stunningly  right idea for the times that we find  ourselves in our politics are polarized  yet we agree on much much more than we  disagree on and so I and I think that if  we were to have rank choice voting in in  our elections that there would be less  of a need to  you know I think that other things will  work themselves out because because of  rank-choice voting but of course  important to get money out of politics  of course you know gerrymandering is is  you know overturning Citizens United  those are things you know that I that I  personally support but I think that  rank-choice voting as a reform will will  necessarily lead to all of the other  reforms because it fix some it fixes  something structural that's beneath  beneath the rest of the politics and  it's sort of it opens things up in a way  it's that's very very powerful it's a  it's a it's a terrific reform we're  really proud of what we did in Maine and  we hope that we pave the way for other  people in other states too to take to  take it on and to run with it and  anybody who's watching you know please  come to our Facebook page the committee  for ranked choice voting and let us know  who you are and and we'll see if we can  we can help you absolutely fantastic  stuff we've been speaking with Kara  Brown McCormick co-founder of the  committee for ranked choice voting in  Maine Kara thank you so much for talking  to me today  thank you David thank you for having me  for getting the word out take care

